<p>U.S. and Iranian negotiators wrapped up another round of Oman-mediated, indirect talks in Geneva on Thursday without announcing a nuclear agreement. Still, Iranian officials and Oman’s mediator said the discussions made progress and would continue. Omani Foreign Minister Badr al-Busaidi called the round “significant progress,” and Iran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi told Iranian media the talks made “very good” progress on both nuclear issues and sanctions relief. The talks are moving forward under heavy pressure. President Trump says he prefers diplomacy but has also warned he will not allow Iran to obtain a nuclear weapon. Multiple outlets also describe a major U.S. military buildup in the Middle East as contingency planning continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/pELTRTnzchH-JuF23Eqgen92nmf3SMsdvjN7sZ9tpvSFPy7NfIurL4VWLVuQtcSe1VD167WRAlZZ9W39lLJzcnKiuexoOLemCW4R25zvVtZbBY4kgba9i3oc3oJ8OqOfUZvVpFRr0uC_Sm-RyABqpAoqcXmL6KIESCdUI-0DXqusEE3E' alt='Iran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi meets Oman’s Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi in Geneva, Feb. 26, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Technical follow-up: Iranian officials said the next phase will move to expert-level work with the U.N. nuclear watchdog in Vienna—a sign the talks may be shifting from broad positions to workable technical parameters.</li><li>Military posture: Reports say the U.S. is boosting its regional presence, including the aircraft carrier USS Gerald R. Ford transiting the Mediterranean. Some outlets described the broader buildup as the largest in more than 20 years.</li><li>Time pressure: Euronews said President Trump gave Tehran 15 days to reach a deal, adding urgency to the Geneva track and the planned near-term follow-on meetings.</li><li>Mixed messaging: Al-Monitor described shifting signals from the Trump administration—alternating between threats and openness to talks—while some Democrats urged consultation with Congress before any military action.</li><li>Sticking points: The Guardian said there is still no clear evidence the sides have narrowed differences on uranium enrichment, even as mediators forecast more talks next week.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Oman (mediator): Foreign Minister Badr al-Busaidi said the sides exchanged “creative and positive ideas,” then later described the round as delivering “significant progress.” He also signaled talks would resume after consultations with capitals. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-02-26-0800/defense-and-security-trump-administration-live-updates-us-and-iran-resume-nuclear-talks-risk-war-looms'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said the talks were “intense” and that both sides engaged “very seriously” on nuclear issues and sanctions. He suggested the next round could come in less than a week. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/27/iran-says-very-good-progress-in-intense-us-talks_6750913_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Trump administration: President Trump said in his State of the Union that he preferred diplomacy but would “never allow” Iran to have a nuclear weapon. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-02-26-0800/defense-and-security-trump-administration-live-updates-us-and-iran-resume-nuclear-talks-risk-war-looms'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Opposition Democrats: Democrats questioned the administration’s objectives and called for Congress to be consulted before the U.S. takes military action, according to Al-Monitor. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/iran-trump-administrations-messaging-mixed'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Analysts/journalists: In an NPR discussion, New York Times journalist David Sanger traced how the standoff developed, the state of Iran’s program, and whether U.S. aims could go beyond nuclear limits to broader goals such as regime change. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/02/26/nx-s1-5727515/is-the-u-s-headed-toward-a-military-conflict-with-iran'>NPR</